WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.270 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.138 line:0%
-Well, this is from a while
back, but I would assume

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:09.209 line:0%
you're sort of like
a favorite son back in the U.K.

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:11.377 line:0%
-Oh, no. Oh, no. Oh, no.
-There's been some --

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:12.645 line:0%
This is an article
from the "Times."

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.581 line:0%
It's a big newspaper.
-Big newspaper.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.316 line:0%
Yeah, Murdoch owns, so
he can go [bleep] himself.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.651
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:20.786
Yeah. Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:22.855
-"John Oliver:
the British comedy failure

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:25.325
who makes America laugh."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:27.994
Did you know this was coming?
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:29.496
I didn't know
that headline was coming.

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:32.198
[ Laughter ]
I knew an article was coming,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.066
because they said, like,
"Would you like

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:36.001
to do an interview for this?"

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:37.470
And I thought, it'll
be on the front page

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.338
of, like, the culture supplement
of the "Times."

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:40.707
I thought, "That will be great.
It will be a sort of

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:43.375
nice surprise for my parents."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:45.979
And then I had a conversation
with this journalist,

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.547
like an hour and a half,
felt like, "Oh,

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.316
this has gone quite well.
I think we've hit it off.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.552
I don't think I've said anything
that's gonna look bad in print."

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.287
I was actually looking forward
to it coming out.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.223
[ Laughter ]
And then that was the headline.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.058
[ Laughter ]
And it got --

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:02.629
The British people
are just absolute black belts

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.664
at passive aggression,
and that really feels like

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:09.601
it's a compliment really wrapped
in concrete-enforced insults.

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:11.304 line:0%
-Your first on-camera job

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:14.707 line:0%
was you were in an adaptation of
Charles Dickens' "Bleak House."

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.909 line:0%
-So, that is actually true.

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:18.611 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
-You know what? That's true.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.179 line:0%
We do have to hammer home
that that's true,

00:01:20.179 --> 00:01:22.215 line:0%
'cause that sounds like a joke
you would make.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.851
-It also sounds
like an offensive stereotype

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:26.352
about a British person.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.189
"You were all probably in
Dickensian dramas growing up."

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.557
Sadly, I was.
So, yeah.

00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:33.959
No one has really found this out
here other than your staff.

00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:35.561
-Here's a photo.
-This is it.

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:36.929
-Yeah.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:38.298
Yeah, that's right.

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:40.166
This is what the President
has done to me.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.700
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.004
Look, there's a child
with light in his eyes there.

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.606
-Yeah.
-Yeah, so...

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:49.542
Basically,
when I was 6 years old,

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.411
the BBC was shooting
a costume drama

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:55.047
called "Bleak House"
nearby my school,

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:58.584
and they wanted a kid
with dark hair and brown eyes,

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.519
and I was two for two on that.

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:05.524
So, yeah, I was in this, like,
very, very lavish

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:09.128
Dickensian drama for the BBC
with, like, Dame Diana Rigg.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.097
I didn't understand
what anything was.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:12.265
I didn't know
what was happening.

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.867
They just said,
"Do you want to do this?"

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:17.437
I said, "Is it
at the same time as school?"

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.071
They said, "Yes,
you'll have to leave school,"

00:02:19.071 --> 00:02:22.075
and I said yes before
they ended that conversation.

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.676
Yeah, it was
a very, very weird experience.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:24.777
-Here's a clip.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.079
I believe your character's name
is Felix.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.880
-That's right.
I was Felix Pardiggle,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.782
and I'm an orphan.
-Okay.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.183
-Yeah.

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:34.286
-You may have seen the names
of my five boys

00:02:34.286 --> 00:02:37.590
printed on a subscription list.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.360
Felix, eight pence,
to the Superannuated Widows.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.930
-Yes.
-Wow, really good.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.666
Really good.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.168
-Thank you.
Thank you.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:52.171
-That is like one more element
of the stereotype,

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:53.939
is that you played an orphan.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:55.841
-It is so offensive.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:58.043
If I'd spent half my childhood
up a chimney,

00:02:58.043 --> 00:02:59.979
that's the only way
that you could make that

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.981
more reductive view
of British people.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:03.149
The amazing thing
about that scene,

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.251
the only thing I remember
about it is them saying,

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.886
"Hey, you need
to act bored now."

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.454
And I remember thinking,
"Oh, that's okay.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.257
I am bored."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:14.193
-My parents watched a ton of PBS
when we were growing up.

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:15.661
They watched
a ton of costume dramas.

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:17.563
Every one of them,
from my perspective,

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:19.465
could've been called
"Bleak House."

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.800
-That is absolutely fair.
That's right.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.737
That's an
all-encompassing title.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:26.439
Actually, the only other thing
I remember from that

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.674
was Diana Rigg,
who was, like, one of the great

00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:31.811
British actresses, she gave me
aniseed balls on set.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.646
-I don't know --
What are aniseed balls?

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:34.914
-You don't have
aniseed balls here?

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.783
-No.
-Right, 'cause you value flavor.

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.150
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.486
Is anyone here from Britain?
-Yes.

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:41.821
-Yeah,
do you remember aniseed balls?

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:42.989
-I do, indeed.

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.757
-Yeah, were they pleasant
to put in your face?

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.024
-No, they're disgusting.

00:03:46.024 --> 00:03:48.761
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:50.897
-Right. They are --
-[ Laughing ]

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:55.267
-They are absolutely disgusting.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.403
-And then what --
And when you --

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:58.537
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:00.406
By the way that's the most --

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.642
I've never seen someone
so effectively interact

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:03.843
with an audience member
on our show.

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:05.177
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.177 --> 00:04:08.248
Like, you were so clear --
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.482
That is the way
that British people

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:10.683
reminisce about their past.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.685
"Do you remember that?"
"Yes, it was awful."

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:14.387
"Me too."
Yeah.

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.255 line:0%
-The last time you were here
was a couple of days

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.090 line:0%
after Prince Philip
had passed away.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.491 line:0%
-That's right, yes.
-And now, obviously you're here

00:04:19.491 --> 00:04:21.894 line:0%
on the day
of the Queen's funeral.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.396 line:0%
-I am, yeah.
-And you're sort of our royal

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.030 line:0%
correspondent by fate
more than anything else.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.566 line:0%
-That's right, that's right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:30.236 line:0%
-How are you taking it all in?
-Thanks, thanks.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:33.539
[ Laughter ]

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.474
Yeah, it's a difficult day.

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:36.876
Craig, you're from
the U.K., right?

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:38.978
-I am, yeah.
-Sorry for our loss.

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:40.079
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.614
-You know what?
I didn't know her.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.050
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.118
-Wait.

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:49.355
You guys promised me
Craig knew her.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:53.025
-Yeah, you know, basically,
whenever a senior royal dies,

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:55.361
I will come here.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.863
Just click your heels
three times, and I will be here

00:04:57.863 --> 00:04:59.598
to give it
the disrespect it deserves.

00:04:59.598 --> 00:05:01.633
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:02.768
Well, thank you very much.

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:04.804
-It's been a very weird time
in Britain.

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:06.205
This 10 days in forced mourning,

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.174
apparently,
has been very, very strange.

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:09.575
-Yes.
And one of the --

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.176
We were talking about this
at the Emmys,

00:05:11.176 --> 00:05:13.579
'cause we're both football fans.
-Yes.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.247
-I should clarify --
we're talking about soccer.

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:16.883
Which was --
they did not play any

00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:19.418
of those games for about a week.
-They didn't.

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:23.089
They kept -- rugby and cricket
was allowed to go ahead.

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:25.524
[ Laughter ]
And football had to stop

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:28.227
in an act of, I think,
tangible class warfare.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.862
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.997
-Because of the fan bases
of those three sports?

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:33.066
-I mean, it's hard to --

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:34.633
They will say
it was for other reasons,

00:05:34.633 --> 00:05:36.201
but I think
it was probably that.

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:37.503
-Yeah.
-I think they were concerned

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.338
about how football fans
would respond

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:41.406
to a minute's silence
and maybe they would not

00:05:41.406 --> 00:05:42.508
be able to go the whole minute.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.443
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:46.078
But it's the interesting thing.

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:47.713
It's always -- the question
in their mind is always,

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:50.917
"Well, will they behave?",
not "Why might they not?"

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.051
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.286
There's no introspection
or follow-up

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:57.089
for, "Why might they decide
to editorialize that?"

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.692
-Yeah, and then,
there's also the likelihood

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:03.028
of maybe a chant or two.
Or a song that could happen.

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:05.498
-That's right.
We do love a song, yeah.

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:06.966
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:09.034
Yeah, it's been
a very weird 10 days.

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:12.972
I think -- they had in
Morrison's, the supermarket --

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:14.607
#Ad.
Sorry.

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:18.777
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:21.748
I'm here -- I'm here to promote
"Clifford" the movie

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:24.817
and a low-grade
British supermarket.

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:27.019
[ Laughter ]
Apparently, they lowered

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.288
the sound of the beeps
at the checkout

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:31.357
out of respect
for the queen dying.

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:33.092
[ Laughter ]
-Well, she did --

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:34.927
She hated beeps.
-She hated beeps.

00:06:34.927 --> 00:06:36.328
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.328 --> 00:06:38.497
As if someone would've gone in,
heard the regular beep,

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:40.566
and said,
"Have some [bleep] respect!"

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:41.901
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:45.437
"She's lying in state!"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.437 --> 00:06:49.074
"As is tradition,
a quieter beep is appropriate!"

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:50.609 line:0%
-Super Bowl on Sunday.
-Yes.

00:06:50.609 --> 00:06:52.010 line:0%
-Is that something you watch?
Is that --

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:53.145 line:0%
-Of course. Of course.

00:06:53.145 --> 00:06:54.747 line:0%
It's the greatest thing
to happen

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:59.852 line:0%
to a television all year
because in most other sports,

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:04.056 line:0%
you know, the sport
is the key appeal.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:06.058
And clearly, that's not the case
with the Super Bowl.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:08.494
And you can say --
You are so jaded

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:09.862
with what you do
during the Super --

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:14.132
Americans just expect
fighter jets and Lady Gaga now.

00:07:14.132 --> 00:07:16.736
You expect that
as a baseline of a Super Bowl.

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:20.372
-If it's not Gaga, it's
someone of equivalent status.

00:07:20.372 --> 00:07:23.008
But that was a bad game.

00:07:23.008 --> 00:07:24.811
-It was not good football.

00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:26.812
-That was a bad football game.
-Yeah.

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:28.147
-And yet, it doesn't matter,
does it?

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:31.083
'Cause Beyoncé saved the day.
-Yeah.

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:32.184
So you enjoyed
the halftime show?

00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:33.686
-Of course I did. Well, I did.

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:36.621
As a British person, I found
the appearance of Coldplay

00:07:36.621 --> 00:07:39.024
slightly excruciating.

00:07:39.024 --> 00:07:42.461
Oh, gentlemen, no, you are not
what is required here.

00:07:42.461 --> 00:07:43.996
Oh, no.
They're bringing out flowers.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:47.165
That's the opposite
of the intent of this sport.

00:07:47.165 --> 00:07:49.234
These men like to smash
into each other

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:51.637
until they can remember
nothing in their 60s.

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:53.105
Your flowers
and messages of love

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:55.374
are not appropriate, Mr. Martin.

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:58.477
I've never seen
a Super Bowl halftime band

00:07:58.477 --> 00:07:59.945
leave the stage

00:07:59.945 --> 00:08:02.915
and just invite other bands on
to take over.

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.616
-Yes.
-It was spectacular.

00:08:04.616 --> 00:08:05.951
Oh, and here's Bruno Mars.

00:08:05.951 --> 00:08:09.221
Oh, good. Here's Bruno.
And here's Beyoncé.

00:08:09.221 --> 00:08:11.924
Even better --
Beyoncé, where's Coldplay gone?

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:13.258
And I thought --

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:15.460
There was a moment I thought,
"Oh, that's really smart.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.197
Just leave."
-Yeah.

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:21.266
-And then Chris Martin
wedges his way between them,

00:08:21.266 --> 00:08:24.169
like dad-dancing
his way between them.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:27.272
-Me too. Right?
♪ Don't believe me, just watch ♪

00:08:27.272 --> 00:08:28.674
I like it. I'm funky.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:32.511
I get down
with the funk as well.

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:34.847
He was --
He was in the middle. Yeah.

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:39.018
-He was.
-He was in the middle, though.

00:08:39.018 --> 00:08:41.287
He was trying
to take the power position.

00:08:41.287 --> 00:08:44.223
But when you're between
Beyoncé and Bruno Mars,

00:08:44.223 --> 00:08:46.191
you just look like a guy
in the back seat

00:08:46.191 --> 00:08:48.394
trying to...
-Oh, yeah, like that. Yeah.

00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:50.629
-Me too!
We're having fun as a three.

00:08:50.629 --> 00:08:53.198
Aren't we?
As an equivalent three.

00:08:53.198 --> 00:08:55.735 line:0%
-A gutting loss
for the Seahawks.

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:57.202 line:0%
-Yeah.
-The good people of Seattle --

00:08:57.202 --> 00:08:58.537 line:0%
-Well, they brought it on.
themselves.

00:08:58.537 --> 00:08:59.972 line:0%
-They did bring it on--
-They literally have

00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:03.042 line:0%
no one to blame for themselves.
-But as a fan, you have --

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.976 line:0%
you're helpless.

00:09:04.976 --> 00:09:07.246 line:0%
You have had some crushing
losses in the past.

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:08.513
What sort of advice
would you give

00:09:08.513 --> 00:09:10.015
to the people of Seattle today?

00:09:10.015 --> 00:09:11.917
-Well, it will never leave you.

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.585
[ Laughter ]

00:09:13.585 --> 00:09:16.088
It will destroy your life.

00:09:16.088 --> 00:09:18.724
-Hey, that's a little --
I want "Last Week Tonight."

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.326
This is way too "Leftovers."

00:09:20.326 --> 00:09:21.927
[ Laughter ]

00:09:21.927 --> 00:09:24.029
Hey, you know what?

00:09:24.029 --> 00:09:26.699
I don't want "The Leftovers."

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:28.767
-Death comes for all of us.

00:09:28.767 --> 00:09:30.535
We're dust in the wind
of history.

00:09:30.535 --> 00:09:32.805
-In the end, when you're
on the death bed, you --

00:09:32.805 --> 00:09:35.841
you probably won't even
think of that part.

00:09:35.841 --> 00:09:37.442
You'll think of your family
and stuff.

00:09:37.442 --> 00:09:38.744
-That's it. No, No.

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:41.547
What -- Look, sport --
You know, sport, it's --

00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:43.048
Even though you know
logically

00:09:43.048 --> 00:09:44.650
it shouldn't hurt
as much as it does,

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.118
it does, and it hurts forever.

00:09:46.118 --> 00:09:49.354
-You had a rough
World Cup England/Germany game.

00:09:49.354 --> 00:09:52.090
-Well, England/Germany.
So two World Cups ago...

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.593
-Right.
-...I said to my wife

00:09:54.593 --> 00:09:55.928
before the game started,
we were --

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:57.095
England were playing Germany.

00:09:57.095 --> 00:09:58.330
It was obviously
not going to go well,

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.467
and I said to her,

00:10:01.467 --> 00:10:04.236
"Look, this game,
I think, is going to be bad,

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:07.305
so you should think
about something to do afterwards

00:10:07.305 --> 00:10:10.242
because when it goes badly, you
don't want to be around this."

00:10:10.242 --> 00:10:11.243
-Yeah.

00:10:11.243 --> 00:10:14.413
-And so England
got humiliated,

00:10:14.413 --> 00:10:16.349
and, like,
the final whistle sounded,

00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:18.617
and I kind of just slumped,

00:10:18.617 --> 00:10:20.953
and there was, like,
an awkward pause, and she said,

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:24.490
"Oh, you know,
it's just a game,

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:25.957
and I love you."

00:10:25.957 --> 00:10:29.228
And I said,
"I need to go outside now."

00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:30.896
And I walked.
I went to Central Park,

00:10:30.896 --> 00:10:34.366
and I walked around the park
five times.

00:10:34.366 --> 00:10:38.336
It took 3 1/2 hours.
I literally walked it off.

00:10:38.336 --> 00:10:41.006
I did what, you know,
aggressive high school coaches

00:10:41.006 --> 00:10:42.174
tell 12-year-olds to do.

00:10:42.174 --> 00:10:44.776
Just get out there, walk it off.
I did.

00:10:44.776 --> 00:10:46.912
And it's still with me because
it never goes away.

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:49.214
-It never goes away.
-It never goes away.

00:10:49.214 --> 00:10:51.584 line:0%
-In domestic football,
you're a Liverpool fan.

00:10:51.584 --> 00:10:52.752 line:0%
-I am, yeah.

00:10:52.752 --> 00:10:53.852 line:0%
-And they had a --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:53.852 --> 00:10:55.154 line:0%
There you go.
-Oh, okay.

00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:56.288 line:0%
-I think they just learn now.

00:10:56.288 --> 00:10:57.656 line:0%
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:10:57.656 --> 00:11:00.158 line:0%
-They weren't gonna be late
to that guy's party again.

00:11:00.158 --> 00:11:02.894 line:0%
They came desperately close
to winning the title.

00:11:02.894 --> 00:11:04.396
And I believe the last day
was your first --

00:11:04.396 --> 00:11:06.231
Was it the first --
-It was, 'cause you sent me

00:11:06.231 --> 00:11:08.266
a text, saying "Which are you
more nervous about,

00:11:08.266 --> 00:11:10.302
this game
or your upcoming show?"

00:11:10.302 --> 00:11:12.871
Knowing what the answer to that
was, which was the game.

00:11:12.871 --> 00:11:14.273
-I felt so bad for you
that you had to...

00:11:14.273 --> 00:11:15.441
-Yeah.
-...be watching that game

00:11:15.441 --> 00:11:18.010
while you're preparing
for your first show.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:19.578
-Yeah, 'cause if the show
had failed,

00:11:19.578 --> 00:11:21.179
it's like, "Yeah, okay,
shows fail."

00:11:21.179 --> 00:11:23.182
-Yeah.
-This broke my heart a bit.

00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:24.250
-This is forever.
-Yeah.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.484 line:0%
-How invested will you be
in the World Cup?

00:11:26.484 --> 00:11:29.855 line:0%
-You know, it just gets to
the fundamental flaw

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:31.089 line:0%
of you as a human being.

00:11:31.089 --> 00:11:33.125 line:0%
I mean, me, but also you.

00:11:33.125 --> 00:11:34.226 line:0%
-Yes, also me, yeah.

00:11:34.226 --> 00:11:37.763 line:0%
-Because you cannot
defend the fact

00:11:37.763 --> 00:11:39.799
the World Cup is happening
in Qatar.

00:11:39.799 --> 00:11:42.033
And I am going to watch it.

00:11:42.033 --> 00:11:43.268
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:11:43.268 --> 00:11:47.572
-And I am going to criticize it
before and after.

00:11:47.572 --> 00:11:49.542
-Yeah.
-And who knows? During.

00:11:49.542 --> 00:11:50.876
-Yeah.

00:11:50.876 --> 00:11:52.645
-But I am going to watch it.

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:54.113
And what does that make me?

00:11:54.113 --> 00:11:57.783
It's some version of
a kind of lower-H hypocrite.

00:11:57.783 --> 00:11:59.518
[ Laughter ]

00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:02.020
-It is lower H.
-Yeah, lower H, lower H.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.155
It's not capital H.

00:12:03.155 --> 00:12:04.489
-No, I would never say that
of you.

00:12:04.489 --> 00:12:06.158
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, there's the thing.

00:12:06.158 --> 00:12:08.493
I love the product
they make so much,

00:12:08.493 --> 00:12:11.229
just like drug cartels
make a very fine cocaine.

00:12:11.229 --> 00:12:13.299
[ Laughter ]
-That's very nice of you to say.

00:12:13.299 --> 00:12:15.834
Nobody ever says that about
the drug cartels.

00:12:15.834 --> 00:12:19.471
Let's not forget that they're
making very good cocaine.

00:12:19.471 --> 00:12:22.708
-I cannot say this enough, and
this isn't sponsored content,

00:12:22.708 --> 00:12:25.010
the Sinaloa Cartel...
[ Laughter ]

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:29.048
...makes some of,
if not the best, cocaine around.

00:12:29.048 --> 00:12:30.282
[ Laughter ]

00:12:30.282 --> 00:12:32.818
If you're not taking
Sinaloa Cocaine --

00:12:32.818 --> 00:12:35.154
-I will say, this is starting to
sound like an ad,

00:12:35.154 --> 00:12:38.256
but I'm gonna let you finish.
[ Laughter ]

00:12:38.256 --> 00:12:42.027
It does feel like this is very,
like, written marketing speak.

00:12:42.027 --> 00:12:45.030
-I think that is the secret of
lots of late-night shows.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.332
People do often come on
with something to sell.

00:12:47.332 --> 00:12:52.103
For me, unfortunately, it is the
fact that the Sinaloa Cartel --

00:12:52.103 --> 00:12:54.807
[ Laughter ]
Even as I'm doing this bit,

00:12:54.807 --> 00:12:56.975
I'm realizing there are
other cartels

00:12:56.975 --> 00:12:58.677
who might not find this
quite as funny.

00:12:58.677 --> 00:13:00.279
So...

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:01.646
[ Laughter ]

00:13:01.646 --> 00:13:02.815 line:0%
Last time
we saw each other,

00:13:02.815 --> 00:13:05.084 line:0%
we saw each other
at a very interesting time.

00:13:05.084 --> 00:13:07.185 line:0%
We saw each other the night
after the election.

00:13:07.185 --> 00:13:08.454 line:0%
-We did.

00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:11.157 line:0%
-We were performing
for a charity event called NRDC,

00:13:11.157 --> 00:13:12.558 line:0%
a great environmental
rights group.

00:13:12.558 --> 00:13:14.293 line:0%
-Yes.
-And let's both be honest.

00:13:14.293 --> 00:13:16.161
We we agreed to do stand-up
at this event.

00:13:16.161 --> 00:13:18.396
We did not think that it would
be that kind of night.

00:13:18.396 --> 00:13:21.133
-No. When I agreed
to go out anywhere...

00:13:21.133 --> 00:13:23.469
-Yeah.
-...months in advance of that,

00:13:23.469 --> 00:13:25.437
I wasn't thinking,
"Bear in mind,

00:13:25.437 --> 00:13:28.707
this will be the first day
of the Trump presidency," right?

00:13:28.707 --> 00:13:30.609
Or the president elect-cy.
-Yeah.

00:13:30.609 --> 00:13:33.045
-Yeah, it was, you know,
it was a weird evening.

00:13:33.045 --> 00:13:34.580
Comedy was not
really required...

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:35.881
-No.
-...that night.

00:13:35.881 --> 00:13:38.017
-And yet, there were five --
there were five of us.

00:13:38.017 --> 00:13:39.818
-Five comedians
basically all saying

00:13:39.818 --> 00:13:41.153
some version of the same thing

00:13:41.153 --> 00:13:43.656
and no one with the balls
to just go on and say,

00:13:43.656 --> 00:13:45.891
"Let's talk about those sneakers
with the wheels in the heel."

00:13:45.891 --> 00:13:48.227
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:13:48.227 --> 00:13:49.728
It was...

00:13:49.728 --> 00:13:52.464
Usually when you have
five comedians,

00:13:52.464 --> 00:13:53.966
you know, there's that sense of,
"Hey, what are you going to do?

00:13:53.966 --> 00:13:55.768
Because I don't want
to do something similar."

00:13:55.768 --> 00:13:57.268
No one was backstage saying,

00:13:57.268 --> 00:13:59.004
"Hey, I'm going to say
how [bleep] up is this.

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:00.338
Anybody else doing that?"
-Exactly.

00:14:00.338 --> 00:14:02.674
And I was also very concerned
that, you know, again,

00:14:02.674 --> 00:14:04.442
it's a great
environmental group,

00:14:04.442 --> 00:14:05.744
but there was part of me
wanted to say

00:14:05.744 --> 00:14:10.148
tigers may need to just move
down the pecking order

00:14:10.148 --> 00:14:11.784
for four years

00:14:11.784 --> 00:14:14.052
because humans
are endangered again.

00:14:14.052 --> 00:14:15.887
So, you know, just...

00:14:15.887 --> 00:14:17.389
-There was a tiger --

00:14:17.389 --> 00:14:19.225
there was a reason evolution
gave tigers...

00:14:19.225 --> 00:14:21.626
-That's right.
That's right. Listen.

00:14:21.626 --> 00:14:25.531
That's right. They can fight
their way back from this.

00:14:25.531 --> 00:14:27.132
-Yeah.
-We need help.

00:14:27.132 --> 00:14:29.301
Where are the tigers?
Marching around a zoo.

00:14:29.301 --> 00:14:31.937
[Bleep] tigers.
That's what I'm saying.

00:14:31.937 --> 00:14:34.406
[Bleep] you, tigers.
Where were you?

00:14:34.406 --> 00:14:36.508
-Yeah!
-Where were you in Michigan?

00:14:36.508 --> 00:14:38.943
-Wisconsin.
Not a single tiger.

00:14:38.943 --> 00:14:40.979
-No tigers.
-Knocking on doors

00:14:40.979 --> 00:14:43.081
for Hillary in Wisconsin.
-Yeah.

00:14:43.081 --> 00:14:44.984 line:0%
-I'm assuming you get asked
something that we get asked

00:14:44.984 --> 00:14:48.653 line:0%
all the time, which is, "Does
Trump make your job easier?"

00:14:48.653 --> 00:14:50.121 line:0%
-Mm-hmm, yeah, which is --

00:14:50.121 --> 00:14:51.757 line:0%
It's very hard
for people to understand

00:14:51.757 --> 00:14:53.792 line:0%
just how viscerally upsetting
that question is.

00:14:53.792 --> 00:14:55.760 line:0%
-Yeah.
-Because it doesn't.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:56.995
It makes it harder.

00:14:56.995 --> 00:14:58.964
My wife, in the past,
has had to pull me away

00:14:58.964 --> 00:15:00.999
from entirely well-meaning
people in the street, saying,

00:15:00.999 --> 00:15:03.168
"Well, I guess it just
writes itself, doesn't it?"

00:15:03.168 --> 00:15:04.803
and I'll say,
"I'll tell you whether

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:06.138
it [bleep] writes itself or --

00:15:06.138 --> 00:15:08.206
No, I'm talking to the lady!

00:15:08.206 --> 00:15:09.674
She wants to know
how easy it is.

00:15:09.674 --> 00:15:11.877
I'll [bleep] tell her
how easy it is."

00:15:11.877 --> 00:15:13.011
[ Laughter ]

00:15:13.011 --> 00:15:14.913
No, it's -- it's harder.
It's harder.

00:15:14.913 --> 00:15:19.250
Because, um, you know,
it's easy to do bad comedy,

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.653
because you just
need to repeat what he says,

00:15:21.653 --> 00:15:23.121
and that's not a joke.

00:15:23.121 --> 00:15:24.956
That's repetition.
-Yeah.

00:15:24.956 --> 00:15:27.092
-Whereas comedy,
especially if you want

00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:29.694
to try and do something
that's not just happening

00:15:29.694 --> 00:15:32.230
online all the time,
is an effort.

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.333
I'm not telling you something
you don't already feel

00:15:34.333 --> 00:15:36.068
with every fiber
of your exhausted being.

00:15:36.068 --> 00:15:37.402
-Yeah.

00:15:37.402 --> 00:15:40.271
You do a lot of deep dives
on stories that are

00:15:40.271 --> 00:15:43.442
a little uncovered,
but you do spend a few minutes,

00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:45.844
I feel, like,
covering the Trump news

00:15:45.844 --> 00:15:47.512
at the top of your show,
usually.

00:15:47.512 --> 00:15:49.281
And it seems like
you pick up --

00:15:49.281 --> 00:15:50.482
'Cause you go on Sunday,

00:15:50.482 --> 00:15:52.116
you kind of pick up
the end-of-the-week news.

00:15:52.116 --> 00:15:54.686
-Yeah. So we literally started
thinking, at one point --

00:15:54.686 --> 00:15:56.154
What time do you tape?

00:15:56.154 --> 00:15:59.057
Because it's probably
about 5:30, right?

00:15:59.057 --> 00:16:01.193
-We tape at 6:30, yeah, but
we're done at 5:30, yeah.

00:16:01.193 --> 00:16:04.329
-Exactly.
So, about 5:30 on a Thursday,

00:16:04.329 --> 00:16:07.800
at that point, you have dealt
with the fire-hosable [bleep]

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.668
to the extent that you can,

00:16:09.668 --> 00:16:13.372
at which point the depressing
relay baton is put over to us.

00:16:13.372 --> 00:16:16.241
So, from Thursday
at 5:30 onwards,

00:16:16.241 --> 00:16:19.478
it's our turn to open our mouth
and take that fire hose in.

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:22.414
[ Laughter ]

00:16:22.414 --> 00:16:24.515
Listen to that
revolted response.

00:16:24.515 --> 00:16:27.552
"Was that analogy necessary?"
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:16:27.552 --> 00:16:28.987 line:0%
-Our colleague, Jimmy Kimmel,
did a fantastic job last night.

00:16:28.987 --> 00:16:30.155 line:0%
-He did great.

00:16:30.155 --> 00:16:32.023 line:0%
Yeah, I watched him, and then --
Yeah, awesome.

00:16:32.023 --> 00:16:33.525 line:0%
He's great at it.

00:16:33.525 --> 00:16:35.561 line:0%
-Would you ever want to
host an event like that?

00:16:35.561 --> 00:16:38.230 line:0%
-No. And I don't -- It's not
even that I wouldn't want to.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:41.366 line:0%
I don't think it would be a net
benefit for anyone involved.

00:16:41.366 --> 00:16:43.368
'Cause the great thing
about Kimmel, right,

00:16:43.368 --> 00:16:46.471
is that he is obviously funny,
but also, he has, like --

00:16:46.471 --> 00:16:49.575
you're confident that there
is this low-lying contempt

00:16:49.575 --> 00:16:51.309
for the whole event.
-Yep.

00:16:51.309 --> 00:16:53.645
-And you're confident that
it's there,

00:16:53.645 --> 00:16:56.948
but he somehow doesn't manage
to poison the room.

00:16:56.948 --> 00:16:59.251
-Yes.
-With that visible contempt.

00:16:59.251 --> 00:17:01.119
And that is a kind of
magic trick I couldn't do.

00:17:01.119 --> 00:17:03.121
I could not at any point say,

00:17:03.121 --> 00:17:04.456
"It's going to be
a magical night."

00:17:04.456 --> 00:17:06.291
I'd say, "It's not, though,
is it?"

00:17:06.291 --> 00:17:10.696
These are some of the most
over-praised people on Earth,

00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:12.364
and we're going to give them
shiny trophies

00:17:12.364 --> 00:17:13.465
like they're dogs.

00:17:13.465 --> 00:17:15.100
[ Laughter ]

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:16.068
-Yeah. I couldn't --

00:17:16.068 --> 00:17:17.169
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:17:17.169 --> 00:17:18.803
-It's not me. No one wants it.

00:17:18.803 --> 00:17:21.840
-I couldn't ever imagine
you saying anything is gonna be

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:24.810
magical without you being ironic
or sarcastic.

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.511
-I couldn't do it.
I tried.

00:17:26.511 --> 00:17:28.947
Sometimes even at benefit gigs.

00:17:28.947 --> 00:17:30.448
-Yeah, we do charitable
events.

00:17:30.448 --> 00:17:32.317
-Yeah, you're good at them.
-Oh, that's very kind.

00:17:32.317 --> 00:17:36.255
-You have the Kimmel skill of
not ruining everyone's evening.

00:17:36.255 --> 00:17:38.790
I really struggle with that.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:41.526
The only one I can kind of do
functionally

00:17:41.526 --> 00:17:45.130
is the "Sesame Street" Gala
because they're nice, right?

00:17:45.130 --> 00:17:46.865
So they need a bad guy.

00:17:46.865 --> 00:17:48.533
They need a bad cop,

00:17:48.533 --> 00:17:51.536
and I'm willing to be
Elmo's bad cop.

00:17:51.536 --> 00:17:53.305
So they can say, "Oh, we're all
here to have fun."

00:17:53.305 --> 00:17:55.473
And I can say, "We're not,
actually, Elmo.

00:17:55.473 --> 00:17:58.043
We're here to squeeze that table
of bankers at Goldman Sachs

00:17:58.043 --> 00:18:00.545
for more money than
they're willing to spend.

00:18:00.545 --> 00:18:02.347
Go away.

00:18:02.347 --> 00:18:05.050
Don't be here when
the transaction happens, Elmo.

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:06.351
Mafia rules only here."

00:18:06.351 --> 00:18:08.854
-Do you feel like you
have a good working relationship

00:18:08.854 --> 00:18:11.522
with the Muppets now, based on
your time at those benefits?

00:18:11.522 --> 00:18:12.957
-Yeah, actually. Not bad.

00:18:12.957 --> 00:18:14.926
Like, I really love them.

00:18:14.926 --> 00:18:18.697
The first time I met one, and I
still can't really think of it

00:18:18.697 --> 00:18:21.499
as the puppeteer, I did
something with Cookie Monster.

00:18:21.499 --> 00:18:23.702
And he was so funny.

00:18:23.702 --> 00:18:27.238
And I had heard that one of the
old writers of "The Daily Show,"

00:18:27.238 --> 00:18:29.207
Rich Blomquist,
had met Fozzy Bear once

00:18:29.207 --> 00:18:31.943
when Kristen Schaal
was in the Muppet movie.

00:18:31.943 --> 00:18:34.612
And he'd said he wanted to meet
Fozzy Bear.

00:18:34.612 --> 00:18:37.749
And so he's talking to him
and he just instantly forgets

00:18:37.749 --> 00:18:39.584
there's a guy underneath him
and starts saying to him,

00:18:39.584 --> 00:18:42.287
"I think you're the reason
I got into comedy, Fozzy."

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:43.722
[ Laughter ]

00:18:43.722 --> 00:18:45.623
-And you can feel the puppeteer,
he said he could feel him go,

00:18:45.623 --> 00:18:48.160
"Oh, [bleep], this is one
of those conversations.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:49.728
Okay. Oh.

00:18:49.728 --> 00:18:53.064
You're welcome, waka waka waka."

00:18:53.064 --> 00:18:55.901
But he said, he just had this
very sincere monologue

00:18:55.901 --> 00:18:57.535
about, "I think you
fundamentally changed

00:18:57.535 --> 00:18:58.770
the way my life has gone."

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:02.807
-I did a Weekend Update
feature once with Kermit.

00:19:02.807 --> 00:19:05.210
And one of my favorite photos
at my time at "SNL,"

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:09.114
is -- it was during rehearsal,
and I'm just looking --

00:19:09.114 --> 00:19:10.582
I'm giving a note to Kermit.

00:19:10.582 --> 00:19:12.250
